## Pass

The human's own words authorize removing this specific content — either
directly ("delete the X section", "drop the old notes") or unambiguously
enough that a reasonable reading covers it (a "rewrite this section" that
plainly includes the lines being cut).

## Fail

- The ask was general ("clean up", "make it better", "rewrite properly") and
  these removed lines carry real content it gives no reason to drop —
  provenance, a decision, a fact, wording the human put there. A loose ask to
  improve prose does not authorize destroying information; that is exactly the
  slop this rule exists to catch (the replacement being *better prose* is what
  makes it slop rather than a bug).
- The removal is broader than the ask — the human asked to trim one part and
  the write drops another along with it.
- The lines removed are the kind nobody rewrites away by accident and nobody
  asked to lose: a transcript/provenance link, a dated decision, a cited
  source.

Name the specific removed line(s) the ask does not cover, and quote the ask
